    "context": "Dryland river waterholes provide critical habitat and serve as refugia for aquatic animals during droughts. However, the quality of these waterholes can often be severely compromised by hypoxic conditions which can lead to mass fish-kills and loss of biodiversity. Understanding how environmental drivers such as temperature and dissolved oxygen shape habitat suitability for aquatic biota under increasing climatic and anthropogenic pressures is crucial in informing river management decisions. Potential questions to explore include a) do habitat suitability vary between sites and fish species? b) how will warming and drying affect habitat suitability? c) what effect does riparian vegetation or other microclimate drivers have on habitat? d) How sensitive are modelled outcomes to different environmental parameters?",
